#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f57a9fc-0051-54e3-9c07-42ef3f9e266e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius and Interval of Convergence of a Power Series when the Limit of the Ratio is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c735412a-f1ac-5bbf-ab67-f6928bd08cf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341635} {:answer/id #uuid "9526e3a3-2617-5b33-917f-7f8cbb5b2700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341636} {:answer/id #uuid "97b167a7-e697-5a29-8bd3-b7cd480ac682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341637} {:answer/id #uuid "8100a30b-463c-5508-9fab-fd71e18f5e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341638} {:answer/id #uuid "11df97b8-5f77-5c77-ab73-96e00961510c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c735412a-f1ac-5bbf-ab67-f6928bd08cf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341635} :answer-field/id #uuid "a93e2f0d-a573-5a5d-9afa-f61b5baafbec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dbb310b5-8ab6-560f-be6f-c09646c87d0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-12409" :question/problem "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{n}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{n+1}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{n}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x{n}^{n}}{(n+1)^{n+1}}| \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{n}}{(n+1)^{n+1}} \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1}{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{n} \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1}{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-n} \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{e(n+1)} \\\\  & =|x|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty ,$ and the interval of convergence is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "beb5fa66-9dc2-50aa-beaa-310da7fc4c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interval of Convergence of a Given Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2d89c84-9ed6-584a-987d-0b414840ae3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-2,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341642} {:answer/id #uuid "8b65f225-e7a1-5e62-855a-2e15f88f728d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-4,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341643} {:answer/id #uuid "41ec57c3-26fd-5cf1-a5af-76b16c4d7c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341644} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb4e5f4-e1c6-569a-81dd-afe843bc2505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341645} {:answer/id #uuid "2d230d80-b719-5f6e-873b-cfd97f329221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d230d80-b719-5f6e-873b-cfd97f329221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341646} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e6cb124-653f-56c6-aee0-f17d3a7d164e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b88949dc-1e89-5760-9055-7f75b5d5164e" :question/math-academy-id "q-125292" :question/problem "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n}(3n-1)}=\\frac{x}{2\\cdot 2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}\\cdot 5}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{2}^{3}\\cdot 8}+⋯\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n}(3n-1)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n+1}(3n+2)}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}(3n-1)}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x(3n-1)}{2(3n+2)}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3n-1}{3n+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{|x|}{2}<1\\,⟹\\,|x|<2.\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $2,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-2,2).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=2,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-2$ and $x=2$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n}(3n-1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{2}^{n}}{{2}^{n}(3n-1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{8}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see if this series converges, we use the limit comparision test, whith ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{3n-1}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.$ We observe that ${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ are positive, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3n-1})}{(\\frac{1}{n})}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{3n-1}=\\frac{1}{3},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is positive and finite. Hence, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ either *both* converge or *both* diverge.\nSince ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}$ is divergent, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3n-1}$ is also divergent by the limit comparison test. So the endpoint $x=2$ is not included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n}(3n-1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-2)^{n}}{{2}^{n}(3n-1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{3n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{8}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So the endpoint $x=-2$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341647 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "beb5fa66-9dc2-50aa-beaa-310da7fc4c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interval of Convergence of a Given Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d595122-48a7-5c03-a850-5adc3f89f80f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341649} {:answer/id #uuid "031481ab-4f8a-54d0-917c-84b0956c1a30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d595122-48a7-5c03-a850-5adc3f89f80f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341649} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a1e632-a787-5b09-9087-0c58519bf79b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341648} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80ce553a-1cae-58f7-9b77-612cb2a460f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80ce553a-1cae-58f7-9b77-612cb2a460f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341652} :answer-field/id #uuid "520b11d9-8590-58e0-8a54-9a6e7a1ec8ab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341651} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c962a34-ec9e-59f0-80f7-a17a7e4c782c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c962a34-ec9e-59f0-80f7-a17a7e4c782c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341654} :answer-field/id #uuid "b411b939-971a-59b1-8350-88e74ead1f64" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da5b4125-dc6e-529e-9a74-bccae1fba186" :question/math-academy-id "q-347514" :question/problem "Find the interval of convergence for the following series:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}=\\frac{x}{4\\cdot 3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}\\cdot 8}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{4}^{3}\\cdot 13}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe interval of convergence is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{4}^{n+1}(5n+3)}\\cdot \\frac{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x(5n-2)}{4(5n+3)}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n-2}{5n+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{|x|}{4}<1\\,⟹\\,|x|<4.\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $4,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-4,4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=4,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-4$ and $x=4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{13}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see if this series converges, we use the limit comparison test, with ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{5n-2}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.$ We observe that ${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ are positive, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5n-2})}{(\\frac{1}{n})}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{5n-2}=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is positive and finite. Hence, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ either *both* converge or *both* diverge.\nSince ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}$ is divergent, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2}$ is also divergent by the limit comparison test. So, the endpoint $x=4$ is not included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4)^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{5n-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{13}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So, the endpoint $x=-4$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-4,4),$ or equivalently, $-4\\le x<4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "beb5fa66-9dc2-50aa-beaa-310da7fc4c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interval of Convergence of a Given Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cdf04bb-c9da-5a8a-bbad-5abbfd354f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cdf04bb-c9da-5a8a-bbad-5abbfd354f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341657} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3420e66-368a-5d24-aef1-32e596cf3648" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341656} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b3d009c-1e50-56b1-8757-edf0ff306b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b3d009c-1e50-56b1-8757-edf0ff306b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341659} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1dc98cb-51eb-56ea-94b9-660fc8f6e86e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341658} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d0eaea2-3a88-5e80-aa11-bcd02d4a08ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d0eaea2-3a88-5e80-aa11-bcd02d4a08ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341661} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d2711d3-7701-5de8-bb96-ff411777ffb7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c89e58c-a9b0-5e19-aea5-3e2d4756af1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-353173" :question/problem "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}=\\frac{2x}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{(2x)^{2}}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{(2x)^{3}}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{(2x)^{4}}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nThe interval of convergence is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}(2x)^{n+1}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-2x(n+4)^{3/2}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+4}{n+5})}^{3/2} \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $2|x|<1,$ or equivalently $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{2},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot (1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that each term of this series ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is smaller than ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\\le \\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nSince ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}$ is convergent, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is convergent by the comparison test. So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}],$ or equivalently, $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f57a9fc-0051-54e3-9c07-42ef3f9e266e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius and Interval of Convergence of a Power Series when the Limit of the Ratio is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39cc58ab-afa8-5144-b261-6ccaf90711a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341664} {:answer/id #uuid "eea0a1b8-f4cb-576d-871d-0b197713d849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341665} {:answer/id #uuid "02e45ab5-2242-5b7f-b5f4-a6583b471163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341666} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9828ca-4ca9-5f10-8c20-61300010dff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341667} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1626cd-994d-557e-9783-d621c96b8616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39cc58ab-afa8-5144-b261-6ccaf90711a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341664} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7ffc27c-01f5-512d-b31d-9168ebf4bf79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0f5b3a4-f463-5be8-8b35-f96396ac3aab" :question/math-academy-id "q-9920" :question/problem "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n+3}}{(2n+3)!}\\cdot \\frac{(2n+1)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1){x}^{2}}{(2n+3)(2n+2)}| \\\\  & =|-{x}^{2}|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n+3)(2n+2)} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty ,$ and the interval of convergence is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}]}
